#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091925 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4149b2fe-4243-595e-af7f-851b308f7eb1" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/id #uuid "c690991e-6029-5067-b97a-67c5e62b7f47" :question/math-academy-id "e-10109" :question/problem "A store has $40$ employees, of which $21$ are female. The store is divided into three departments, $A, B,$ and $C.$ There are $7$ female workers in department $A,$ while there are $8$ female workers in department $C.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected store worker is a female that works in department $B?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that the chosen worker is a female, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected worker works department $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected worker works department $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected worker works department $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A \\sqcup B \\sqcup C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nRecall that the law of total probability states that\n\n$$\nP(F) = P(F \\cap A) + P(F \\cap B) + P(F \\cap C).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find $P(F \\cap B)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- To calculate $P(F \\cap A),$ we take the number of females who work in department $A$ $(7)$ and divide by the total number of workers $(40).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(F \\cap A) = \\dfrac{7}{40}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(F \\cap C),$ we take the number of females who work in department $C$ $(8)$ and divide by the total number of workers $(40).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(F \\cap C) = \\dfrac{8}{40}.\n$$\n\n- Also, we're given that\n\n$$\nP(F) = \\dfrac{21}{40}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(F \\cap B),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F) & =P(F\\cap A)+P(F\\cap B)+P(F\\cap C) \\\\ \\frac{21}{40} & =\\frac{7}{40}+P(F\\cap B)+\\frac{8}{40} \\\\ P(F\\cap B) & =\\frac{21}{40}-\\frac{7}{40}-\\frac{8}{40}. \\\\ P(F\\cap B) & =\\frac{6}{40} \\\\ P(F\\cap B) & =\\frac{3}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091926 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96f54294-8bb4-5338-969b-76c8d1536daa" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Law of Total Probability in Terms of Conditional Probability"}] :question/id #uuid "1104f073-7f90-51ce-9e95-aae0935ca852" :question/math-academy-id "e-10110" :question/problem "Three factories produce the same spare part for a particular car model. Factory ${A}$ produces $32\\%$ of the overall parts, of which $1.5\\%$ are defective. Factory ${B}$ produces $33\\%$ of the overall parts, of which $2\\%$ are defective, and Factory ${C}$ produces $35\\%$ of the overall parts, of which $1.4\\%$ are defective. If one car part is selected randomly, what is the probability that it is defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly selected part is defective, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected part was produced by Factory ${A},$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected part was produced by Factory ${B},$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected part was produced by Factory ${C}.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A \\sqcup B \\sqcup C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected part is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since Factory ${A}$ produces $32\\%$ of the parts, Factory ${B}$ produces $33 \\%$ of the parts, and Factory ${C}$ produces $35 \\%$ of the parts, we have\n\n$$\nP(A) = 0.32, \\qquad P(B) = 0.33, \\qquad P(C)=0.35.\n$$\n\n- Since $1.5\\%$ of the parts produced by Factory ${A},$ $2\\%$ of the parts produced by Factory ${B},$ and $1.4\\%$ of the parts produced by Factory ${C}$ are defective, we have \n\n$$\nP(D | A) = 0.015, \\qquad P(D | B) = 0.02, \\qquad P(D | C) = 0.014.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected part is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(0.015)(0.32)+(0.02)(0.33)+(0.014)(0.35) \\\\  & =0.004\\,8+0.006\\,6+0.004\\,9 \\\\  & =0.016\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091927 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0ebe700-63af-57d4-ae8f-9e09dac5cf76" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional Probability Using the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/id #uuid "ae39bb45-ac10-562a-bac6-4f192c5f0aed" :question/math-academy-id "e-10111" :question/problem "Three baskets, $A, B,$ and $C,$ contain apples. It is known that $\\dfrac25$ of the apples in basket $A$ and $\\dfrac14$ of the apples in basket $B$ are red. A basket is selected at random, and an apple is randomly chosen from the basket. If the probability that the selected apple is red is $\\dfrac13,$ what proportion of the apples from basket $C$ are red?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the event that the selected apple is red, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected apple is from basket ${A},$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected apple is from basket ${B},$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected apple is from basket ${C}.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A \\sqcup B \\sqcup C$ covers the entire sample space. \n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a red apple, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a basket is randomly selected and there are $3$ baskets, we have\n\n$$\nP(A) = P(B) = P(C) = \\dfrac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\dfrac25$ of the apples in basket $A$ and $\\dfrac14$ of the apples in basket $B$ are red, we have\n\n$$\nP(R | A)= \\dfrac25, \\qquad P(R | B) = \\dfrac14.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of picking a red apple from any of the three baskets is $\\dfrac13,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(R) = \\dfrac13.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(R|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =(\\frac{2}{5})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{4})(\\frac{1}{3})+P(R|C)(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{2}{15}+\\frac{1}{12}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{15}-\\frac{1}{12} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) & =\\frac{7}{60} \\\\ P(R|C) & =\\frac{7}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\dfrac{7}{20}$ of the apples from basket $C$ are red."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96f54294-8bb4-5338-969b-76c8d1536daa" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Law of Total Probability in Terms of Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a8f3c09-7e04-5929-b00d-c456ae1b0cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089520} {:answer/id #uuid "e514d21e-1875-5fe4-8b93-49e157f947e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089521} {:answer/id #uuid "221c8420-b5cd-5569-b795-b7ccfd431dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.036" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089522} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9ac93d-5083-50fe-bbbd-7d41f4eaed85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.061" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089523} {:answer/id #uuid "541c4fe1-8053-5f25-9ec7-856be1402627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a9ac93d-5083-50fe-bbbd-7d41f4eaed85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.061" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089523} :answer-field/id #uuid "8108017c-0046-55ab-abca-dff7fc26c853" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f910c2f8-2684-5671-bb61-e8e8d6c2b119" :question/math-academy-id "q-143529" :question/problem "A company manufactures electronic components. Of the components manufactured, $45%$ are produced by the first machine, $25%$ are produced by the second machine, and the third machine produces the remaining.\nIf $8%$ of the components produced by the first machine, $4%$ produced by the second machine, and $5%$ produced by the third machine are defective, what is the probability that a randomly selected component is defective?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly selected component is defective, and \n\n- let ${B}_{1}$ be the event that the component was produced by the first machine,\n\n- let ${B}_{2}$ be the event that the component was produced by the second machine,\n\n- let ${B}_{3}$ be the event that the component was produced by the third machine.\n\nThen the disjoint union ${B}_{1}⊔{B}_{2}⊔{B}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap {B}_{1})+P(D\\cap {B}_{2})+P(D\\cap {B}_{3}) \\\\  & =P(D|{B}_{1})P({B}_{1})+P(D|{B}_{2})P({B}_{2})+P(D|{B}_{3})P({B}_{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since the first machine produces $45%$ of the components, the second machine produces $25%$ of the components, the third machine produces the remaining\n\n$$\n100%-(45%+25%)=30%.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nP({B}_{1})=0.45,\\,P({B}_{2})=0.25,\\,P({B}_{3})=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $8%$ of the components produced by the first machine, $4%$ produced by the second machine, and $5%$ produced by the third machine are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|{B}_{1})=0.08,\\,P(D|{B}_{2})=0.04,\\,P(D|{B}_{3})=0.05.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|{B}_{1})P({B}_{1})+P(D|{B}_{2})P({B}_{2})+P(D|{B}_{3})P({B}_{3}) \\\\  & =(0.08)(0.45)+(0.04)(0.25)+(0.05)(0.3) \\\\  & =0.036+0.01+0.015 \\\\  & =0.061.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4149b2fe-4243-595e-af7f-851b308f7eb1" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "215b87a6-81fc-5cab-a694-78aa0095ae2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436149} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f14c54-9815-5d63-bc8b-4867f5bb4db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436190} {:answer/id #uuid "bab1c9d3-861d-5fc5-a92c-ad4cd019e1e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436192} {:answer/id #uuid "d17bdea3-83a8-52df-9f99-77630fda70c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436198} {:answer/id #uuid "d32e28b1-e396-5963-b787-cedd10424d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0f14c54-9815-5d63-bc8b-4867f5bb4db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436190} :answer-field/id #uuid "809d8147-8c94-5f8b-b479-0c8092abc324" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8bdf577-59f8-5a92-b08a-72c7b5bf639b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143560" :question/problem "Among the people arrested in a particular city in 2010, $16%$ were males under $18,$ $30%$  were males between $18$ and $40,$ and $24%$ were males over $40.$ What is the probability that a person randomly selected from all those arrested in 2010 is male?" :question/worked-solution "Let $M$ be the event that a person selected was a male, and\n\n- let ${A}_{1}$ be the event that the person was under $18$,\n\n- let ${A}_{2}$ be the event that the person was between $18$ and $40$, and\n\n- let ${A}_{3}$ be the event that the person was over $40.$\n\nThen the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected person is male, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(M)=P(M\\cap {A}_{1})+P(M\\cap {A}_{2})+P(M\\cap {A}_{3})\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nP(M\\cap {A}_{1})=0.16,\\,P(M\\cap {A}_{2})=0.3\\,P(M\\cap {A}_{3})=0.24.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected person is male is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M) & =P(M\\cap {A}_{1})+P(M\\cap {A}_{2})+P(M\\cap {A}_{3}) \\\\  & =0.16+0.3+0.24 \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96f54294-8bb4-5338-969b-76c8d1536daa" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Law of Total Probability in Terms of Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "306c6c6b-a3f0-535f-b65f-c01df8a0c6a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.041" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436151} {:answer/id #uuid "315b5ce4-cdef-5a05-900d-d09c2a07e7be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.342" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436153} {:answer/id #uuid "706804b2-6f19-5053-8f45-588b0942a4eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.051" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436176} {:answer/id #uuid "95bd2b23-5128-5cab-b41d-ef4058310976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436183} {:answer/id #uuid "a2068431-9034-514f-adff-ba0037c20a7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.043" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2068431-9034-514f-adff-ba0037c20a7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.043" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436186} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7e78d0f-3ddc-5e29-ac7f-d4654fbb2dea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "507f5970-478a-564c-a05d-7f723ba11f01" :question/math-academy-id "q-143626" :question/problem "A company manufactures three types of cars. Of the car population, $40%$ are model ${C}_{1},$  $35%$ are model ${C}_{2},$ and the remaining cars are model ${C}_{3}.$ If $6%$ of the model ${C}_{1},$ $4%$ of the model  ${C}_{2},$ and $2%$ of the model ${C}_{3}$ cars break down during the first year, what is the probability that a randomly selected car will break down during the first year?" :question/worked-solution "Let $B$ be the event that a randomly selected car breaks down during the first year, and \n\n- let ${C}_{1}$ be the event that the car is of model ${C}_{1},$\n\n- let ${C}_{2}$ be the event that the car is of model ${C}_{2},$\n\n- let ${C}_{3}$ be the event that the car is of model ${C}_{3}.$\n\nThen the disjoint union ${C}_{1}⊔{C}_{2}⊔{C}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected car will break down during the first year, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B) & =P(B\\cap {C}_{1})+P(B\\cap {C}_{2})+P(B\\cap {C}_{3}) \\\\  & =P(B|{C}_{1})P({C}_{1})+P(B|{C}_{2})P({C}_{2})+P(B|{C}_{3})P({C}_{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $40%$ of the cars produced by the company are model ${C}_{1},$ and $50%$ are model ${C}_{2},$  the model ${C}_{3}$ cars make up the remaining\n\n$$\n100%-(40%+35%)=25%.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nP({C}_{1})=0.4,\\,P({C}_{2})=0.35,\\,P({C}_{3})=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $6%$ of the cars of model ${C}_{1},$ $4%$ of those of model  ${C}_{2},$ and $2%$ of the cars of model ${C}_{3}$ break down during the first year, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|{C}_{1})=0.06,\\,P(B|{C}_{2})=0.04,\\,P(B|{C}_{3})=0.02.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected car breaks down during the first year is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B) & =P(B|{C}_{1})P({C}_{1})+P(B|{C}_{2})P({C}_{2})+P(B|{C}_{3})P({C}_{3}) \\\\  & =(0.06)(0.4)+(0.04)(0.35)+(0.02)(0.25) \\\\  & =0.024+0.014+0.005 \\\\  & =0.043.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4149b2fe-4243-595e-af7f-851b308f7eb1" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eeceee9-6b7e-5700-a74d-8554e7f70cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514943} {:answer/id #uuid "623f5887-d90a-5fd9-995d-57c77b99a09d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515085} {:answer/id #uuid "b545930d-e0e3-5eee-bcf2-24a05aca55ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515252} {:answer/id #uuid "d0fd87a2-4baf-59fc-a98d-12d7d2b4e955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515310} {:answer/id #uuid "e9dd86a2-28a9-54a1-bd47-96969ce92071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9dd86a2-28a9-54a1-bd47-96969ce92071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515365} :answer-field/id #uuid "b160229e-63c8-5bb3-85c1-a2c73ec30a06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1ef773a-e0ea-5eef-bbf3-332e3e7efe2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143628" :question/problem "Mike has a total of $100$ candies divided into three bags. The first bag contains $18$ mint-flavored candies, while the second bag contains $16$ mint-flavored candies. If $55%$ of all of Mike's candies are mint-flavored, what is the probability that a randomly selected candy is a mint-flavored candy from the third bag?" :question/worked-solution "Let $M$ be the event that the selected candy is mint-flavored, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected candy is from the first bag,\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected candy is from the second bag,\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected candy is from the third bag.\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nRecall that the law of total probability states that\n\n$$\nP(M)=P(M\\cap A)+P(M\\cap B)+P(M\\cap C).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find $P(M\\cap C):$\n\n- To calculate $P(M\\cap A),$ we take the number of mint-flavored candies in the first bag $(18)$ and divide by the total number of candies $(100).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(M\\cap A)=\\frac{18}{100}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(M\\cap B),$ we take the number of mint-flavored candies in the second bag $(16)$ and divide by the total number of candies$(100).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(M\\cap B)=\\frac{16}{100}.\n$$\n\n- Also, we're given that\n\n$$\nP(M)=55%=\\frac{55}{100}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(M\\cap C),$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M) & =P(M\\cap A)+P(M\\cap B)+P(M\\cap C) \\\\ \\frac{55}{100} & =\\frac{18}{100}+\\frac{16}{100}+P(M\\cap C) \\\\ P(M\\cap C) & =\\frac{55}{100}-\\frac{18}{100}-\\frac{16}{100} \\\\ P(M\\cap C) & =\\frac{21}{100} \\\\ P(M\\cap C) & =21%\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515595 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4149b2fe-4243-595e-af7f-851b308f7eb1" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "021e9a8f-b7c8-5617-b841-181c6dabad37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{1\\,500}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514877} {:answer/id #uuid "13f621be-7da4-57ec-be1a-3bb71fd27cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{1\\,100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514915} {:answer/id #uuid "70fa78ce-a5bb-554d-89bf-e9ff2f2c268f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{900}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515103} {:answer/id #uuid "7c0bd94e-0737-59fd-971d-a9571075b9e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{800}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515129} {:answer/id #uuid "b33a5833-4704-5b74-8d07-51d290832266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{1\\,000}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b33a5833-4704-5b74-8d07-51d290832266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{1\\,000}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515246} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ddb04e3-1a47-50bb-8bb5-20ad79469f4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3e46311-f49f-5429-9145-049194bfe241" :question/math-academy-id "q-143630" :question/problem "A certain disease is present in $500$ out of $100\\,000$ people in city $A,$ $800$ out of $200\\,000$ people in city $B,$ and $1\\,100$ out of $500\\,000$ people in city $C.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected person from any city has the disease?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that a person has the disease, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the person is from city $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the person is from city $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the person is from city $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected person has the disease, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D)=P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C)\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that there are a total of \n\n$$\n100\\,000+200\\,000+500\\,000=800\\,000\n$$\n\npeople in all three cities.\n\n- To calculate $P(D\\cap A),$ we take the number of people in city $A$ who have the disease $(500)$ and divide by the total number of people in the three cities $(800\\,000).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(D\\cap A)=\\frac{500}{800\\,000}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(D\\cap B),$ we take the number of people in city $B$ who have the disease $(800)$ and divide by the total number of people in the three cities $(800\\,000).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(D\\cap B)=\\frac{800}{800\\,000}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(D\\cap C),$ we take the number of people in city $C$ who have the disease $(1\\,100)$ and divide by the total number of people in the three cities $(800\\,000).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(D\\cap C)=\\frac{1\\,100}{800\\,000}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected person from any city has the disease is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =\\frac{500}{800\\,000}+\\frac{800}{800\\,000}+\\frac{1\\,100}{800\\,000} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\,400}{800\\,000} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{8\\,000} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1\\,000}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4149b2fe-4243-595e-af7f-851b308f7eb1" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17b422f9-8fe0-59bc-9eec-efa1f37dfae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514924} {:answer/id #uuid "1a9d603e-b926-5237-8790-115938fc5ec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514931} {:answer/id #uuid "80f55f6c-60e9-5aa3-80c4-2b3c42adb117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515135} {:answer/id #uuid "94008e65-8008-5f2f-98cd-a9a293d66546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515185} {:answer/id #uuid "cd730609-69cd-5dce-ba00-d82c5d65d5ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a9d603e-b926-5237-8790-115938fc5ec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514931} :answer-field/id #uuid "9aa2967b-23f5-554f-85ed-580439a3657b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c96dcfa-0dd7-50ec-b775-86d1b3c50f89" :question/math-academy-id "q-143722" :question/problem "A total of $200$ colored table tennis balls are distributed among three boxes, $A,$ $B,$ and $C.$ Box $A$ contains $20$ green balls, while Box $B$ contains $30$ green balls. If $45%$ of all the balls are green, what is the probability that a randomly selected ball is a green ball from Box $C?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $G$ be the event that the selected ball is green, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected ball is from box $A$,\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected ball is from box $B$,\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected ball is from box $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nRecall that the law of total probability states that\n\n$$\nP(G)=P(G\\cap A)+P(G\\cap B)+P(G\\cap C).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find $P(G\\cap C):$\n\n- To calculate $P(G\\cap A),$ we take the number of green balls in box $A$ $(20)$ and divide by the total number of balls $(200).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(G\\cap A)=\\frac{20}{200}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(G\\cap B),$ we take the number of green balls in box $B$ $(30)$ and divide by the total number of balls $(200).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(G\\cap B)=\\frac{30}{200}.\n$$\n\n- Also, we're given that\n\n$$\nP(G)=45%=\\frac{90}{200}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(G\\cap C),$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G\\cap A)+P(G\\cap B)+P(G\\cap C) \\\\ \\frac{90}{200} & =\\frac{20}{200}+\\frac{30}{200}+P(G\\cap C) \\\\ P(G\\cap C) & =\\frac{90}{200}-\\frac{20}{200}-\\frac{30}{200} \\\\ P(G\\cap C) & =\\frac{40}{200} \\\\ P(G\\cap C) & =20%\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4149b2fe-4243-595e-af7f-851b308f7eb1" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1feaa879-4a53-577a-ac18-96df41141043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436148} {:answer/id #uuid "33008623-0338-5660-826c-0c65d608c914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436155} {:answer/id #uuid "59062f7d-4e46-50d5-8afd-8f68d15b2115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436167} {:answer/id #uuid "d3199c3b-4f96-548c-963e-232640c914ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436199} {:answer/id #uuid "e24464ec-be29-5034-8294-e920ccc39cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e24464ec-be29-5034-8294-e920ccc39cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436201} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ad09311-474c-5682-b225-5468fda63640" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3be4391a-3aa5-5bc8-9302-e0ef613d626c" :question/math-academy-id "q-143723" :question/problem "Among all the people that visited Bill's ice cream shop last weekend, $25%$ were females under $18,$ $20%$ were females between $18$ and $30,$ and $15%$ were females over $30.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected person who visited Bill's shop last weekend is female?" :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that a person selected was a female.\n\n- let ${A}_{1}$ be the event that the person was under $18$,\n\n- let ${A}_{2}$ be the event that the person was at least  $18$ but under $30$,\n\n- let ${A}_{3}$ be the event that the person was at least $30$ or older.\n\nThen the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected person was a female, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(F)=P(F\\cap {A}_{1})+P(F\\cap {A}_{2})+P(F\\cap {A}_{3})\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nP(F\\cap {A}_{1})=0.25,\\,P(F\\cap {A}_{2})=0.2\\,P(F\\cap {A}_{3})=0.15.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected person is a female is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F) & =P(F\\cap {A}_{1})+P(F\\cap {A}_{2})+P(F\\cap {A}_{3}) \\\\  & =0.25+0.2+0.15 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96f54294-8bb4-5338-969b-76c8d1536daa" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Law of Total Probability in Terms of Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05abde05-5ea0-5346-b70b-e15ddb38a2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436144} {:answer/id #uuid "18474b78-45ed-57a3-8328-b554a713c479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436147} {:answer/id #uuid "233072a2-5bde-566b-9863-22d8af3afca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436150} {:answer/id #uuid "5c42dd93-109c-5ae0-8799-e0b629f2949b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436168} {:answer/id #uuid "60c59049-514f-5905-b9eb-4d7a362f5ce6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05abde05-5ea0-5346-b70b-e15ddb38a2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436144} :answer-field/id #uuid "85fd5ffd-e434-5aa7-93f5-7f4265ffa862" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca1787dc-fa3e-5ab1-99c5-cbfbd849d0c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-143747" :question/problem "Three bags contain marbles. Bag $1$ contains $12$ red marbles and $8$ green marbles. Bag $2$ contains $14$ red marbles and $6$ green marbles. And bag $3$ contains $10$ red marbles and $10$ green marbles. A bag is selected at random, and a marble is randomly selected from the bag. What is the probability that the marble is red?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the event of selecting a red marble at random from one of the three bags, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the marble is from bag $1,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the marble is from bag $2,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the marble is from bag $3.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a red marble at random from one of the three bags, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a bag is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since there are $12$ red marbles out of $20$ in bag $1,$ $14$ red marbles out of $20$ in bag $2,$ and $10$ red marbles out of $20$  in bag $3,$ we have \n\n$$\nP(R|A)=\\frac{12}{20},\\,P(R|B)=\\frac{14}{20},\\,P(R|C)=\\frac{10}{20}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability of selecting a red marble at random from one of the three bags is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(\\frac{12}{20})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{14}{20})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{10}{20})(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{60}+\\frac{14}{60}+\\frac{10}{60} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{60} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96f54294-8bb4-5338-969b-76c8d1536daa" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Law of Total Probability in Terms of Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30da36d7-24a3-55da-be75-a03f2fba54fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.152" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436152} {:answer/id #uuid "357f0b32-a56b-52d7-95ec-1d35f74b7cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.027" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436157} {:answer/id #uuid "573e601d-0da3-5909-a0c9-3846969898b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.211" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436166} {:answer/id #uuid "60025f2b-4528-5cf1-84f8-269bccbd14fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.085" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436171} {:answer/id #uuid "f9341cb2-5d0e-586a-890f-9d8f2bd36dd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.421" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "357f0b32-a56b-52d7-95ec-1d35f74b7cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.027" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436157} :answer-field/id #uuid "c21336b1-0625-5280-8d3b-20f566b7feef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb95f880-a6d7-5a6d-8e15-40d3ec490c19" :question/math-academy-id "q-143749" :question/problem "Three companies supply widgets to a car shop. Company $A$ supplies $45%$ of the widgets, of which $2%$ are defective. Company $B$ provides $40%$ of the widgets, of which  $3%$ are defective. And company $C$ supplies the remaining widgets, of which $4%$ are defective. What is the probability that a randomly chosen widget from these companies will turn out to be defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that the widget is defective, and  \n\n- let $A$ be that the event that the widget was produced by company $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be that the event  that the widget was produced by company $B$,\n\n- let $C$ be that the event that the widget was produced by company $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.  \n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly  selected widget  is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since Company $A$ supplies $45%$ and Company $B$ produces $40%$ of the widgets, then Company $B$ produces the remaining\n\n$$\n100%-(45%+40%)=15%.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.45,\\,P(B)=0.4,\\,P(C)=0.15.\n$$\n\n- Since $2%$ of the widgets produced by Company $A$, $3%$ of the widgets produced by Company $B$, and $4%$ of the widgets produced by Company $C,$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.02,\\,P(D|B)=0.03,\\,P(D|C)=0.04.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected widget turns out to be defective is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(0.02)(0.45)+(0.03)(0.4)+(0.04)(0.15) \\\\  & =0.009+0.012+0.006 \\\\  & =0.027.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96f54294-8bb4-5338-969b-76c8d1536daa" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Law of Total Probability in Terms of Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09666b40-5355-57b1-8b42-60c63995a1c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.030" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514890} {:answer/id #uuid "1f8ba24e-a713-55d8-8ec1-c3ba12f16e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.035" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514944} {:answer/id #uuid "83b5f13c-3fd2-5d10-b864-2eb89194f1c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.116" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515144} {:answer/id #uuid "89585630-1207-54f3-a6dd-d0dda6b0d569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.191" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515157} {:answer/id #uuid "ce33935e-f5d0-55b5-9281-89fcc88c0258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.233" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89585630-1207-54f3-a6dd-d0dda6b0d569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.191" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515157} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed1fa99e-a491-5eba-bafa-dde6be00551b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5295db33-6fb5-59ca-9a76-f6dda26f7e03" :question/math-academy-id "q-143752" :question/problem "In a particular park, there are three forests. Forest $A$ occupies $40%$ of the park area, and  $30%$ of its plants are poisonous. Forest $B$ occupies $25%$ of the park area, and $20%$ of its plants are poisonous. Forest $C$ occupies the remaining park area, and $6%$ of its plants are poisonous. What is the probability that a randomly selected plant from this park is poisonous? Assume that the plants are evenly distributed throughout the park." :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the event that the plant is poisonous, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the plant is from forest $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the plant is from forest $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the plant is from forest $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected plant is poisonous, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(V) & =P(V\\cap A)+P(V\\cap B)+P(V\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(V|A)P(A)+P(V|B)P(B)+P(V|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since forest $A$ occupies $40%$ of the park area and forest $B$ occupies $25%$, then forest $C$ occupies the remaining\n\n$$\n100%-(40%+25%)=35%.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.4,\\,P(B)=0.25,\\,P(C)=0.35.\n$$\n\n- Since $30%$ of the plants in forest $A,$ $20%$ of the plants in forest $B$, and $6%$ of the plants in forrest $C$ are poisonous, we have\n\n$$\nP(V|A)=0.3,\\,P(V|B)=0.2,\\,P(V|C)=0.06.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected plant in that park is poisonous is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(V) & =P(V|A)P(A)+P(V|B)P(B)+P(V|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(0.3)(0.4)+(0.2)(0.25)+(0.06)(0.35) \\\\  & =0.12+0.05+0.021 \\\\  & =0.191.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0ebe700-63af-57d4-ae8f-9e09dac5cf76" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional Probability Using the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "488a3705-98d3-5a24-9004-41ab9c1c321c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.2%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436162} {:answer/id #uuid "6a530138-b638-5639-a711-718842186f49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.6%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436174} {:answer/id #uuid "87ddb4d4-dca4-56d7-900e-03e65bd00020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.6%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436179} {:answer/id #uuid "93d317ba-d06f-53ee-9594-becc9ecb5ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436181} {:answer/id #uuid "9a8da790-3e42-5a7f-b4b2-a5fd942dcb65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93d317ba-d06f-53ee-9594-becc9ecb5ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436181} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b8dcb20-7e9f-5dac-bfdc-4def0a6201e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cad3485a-4b9b-57e5-9ec4-43f98bf6be7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143877" :question/problem "A company manufactures three types of cars. Of the total car population, $40%$ are model ${M}_{1},$ $35%$ are model ${M}_{2},$ and $25%$ are model ${M}_{3}.$ If $6%$ of model ${M}_{1}$ and $4%$ of model ${M}_{2}$ cars break down during the first year, and the probability that a randomly selected car from any of the three models breaks down during the first year is $0.045,$ what percentage of model ${M}_{3}$ cars will break down during the first year?" :question/worked-solution "Let $B$ be the event that a randomly selected car breaks down during the first year, and \n\n- let ${M}_{1}$ be the event that the car is model ${M}_{1},$\n\n- let ${M}_{2}$ be the event that the car is model ${M}_{2},$\n\n- let ${M}_{3}$ be the event that the car is model ${M}_{3}.$\n\nThen the disjoint union ${M}_{1}⊔{M}_{2}⊔{M}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected car will break down during the first year, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B) & =P(B\\cap {M}_{1})+P(B\\cap {M}_{2})+P(B\\cap {M}_{3}) \\\\  & =P(B|{M}_{1})P({M}_{1})+P(B|{M}_{2})P({M}_{2})+P(B|{M}_{3})P({M}_{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $40%$ of cars are model ${M}_{1},$, $35%$ are model ${M}_{2}$, and $25%$ are model ${M}_{3},$ we have\n\n$$\nP({M}_{1})=0.4,\\,P({M}_{2})=0.35,\\,P({M}_{3})=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $6%$ of model ${M}_{1}$ and $4%$ of model  ${M}_{2}$ break down during the first year, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|{M}_{1})=0.06,\\,P(B|{M}_{2})=0.04.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected car breaks down during the first year is $0.045,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=0.045.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(B|{M}_{3}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B) & =P(B|{M}_{1})P({M}_{1})+P(B|{M}_{2})P({M}_{2})+P(B|{M}_{3})P({M}_{3}) \\\\ 0.045 & =(0.06)(0.4)+(0.04)(0.35)+P(B|{M}_{3})(0.25) \\\\ 0.045 & =0.024+0.014+0.25P(B|{M}_{3}) \\\\ 0.25P(B|{M}_{3}) & =0.007 \\\\ P(B|{M}_{3}) & =0.028=2.8%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2.8%$ of model ${M}_{3}$ cars break down during the first year."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0ebe700-63af-57d4-ae8f-9e09dac5cf76" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional Probability Using the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0506ec88-a559-5e5a-a458-6fc52ef5c6ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514885} {:answer/id #uuid "3acf24ec-8a67-54b8-9448-b5fcd88660d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515001} {:answer/id #uuid "49850079-8fcd-5314-a588-f2594bdc6cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515034} {:answer/id #uuid "53c3616a-163e-56aa-81e3-423c9687314d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515054} {:answer/id #uuid "fa17c92e-61ad-528a-bb3c-cafbd0c254a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3acf24ec-8a67-54b8-9448-b5fcd88660d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515001} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffe4c3f0-bc0e-50f0-bcdc-1b3ec7c8a111" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6838f2f1-19a5-5913-bfcd-14e938b0311d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143902" :question/problem "Three bags, $A,B,$ and $C,$ contain marbles. Bag $A$ contains $12$ red and $8$ green marbles, while bag $B$ contains $14$ red and $6$ green marbles. A bag is selected at random, and a marble is randomly selected from the bag. If the probability that the selected marble is green is $\\frac{2}{5},$  what proportion of marbles from bag $C$ are green?" :question/worked-solution "Let $G$ be the event of selecting a green marble at random from one of the three bags, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the marble is from bag $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the marble is from bag $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the marble is from bag $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space. \n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a green marble, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G\\cap A)+P(G\\cap B)+P(G\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|B)P(B)+P(G|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a bag is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since there are $8$ green marbles out of $20$  in bag $A,$ and  $6$ green marbles out of $20$  in bag $B,$ we have \n\n$$\nP(G|A)=\\frac{8}{20},\\,P(G|B)=\\frac{6}{20}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of picking a green marble  from one of the bags is $\\frac{2}{5},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G)=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(G|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|B)P(B)+P(G|C)P(C) \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & =(\\frac{8}{20})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{6}{20})(\\frac{1}{3})+P(G|C)(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & =\\frac{2}{15}+\\frac{1}{10}+(\\frac{1}{3})P(G|C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(G|C) & =\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{15}-\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(G|C) & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ P(G|C) & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{1}{2}$ of the marbles from bag $C$ are green."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0ebe700-63af-57d4-ae8f-9e09dac5cf76" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional Probability Using the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07a8bb0b-fd79-5284-a3d7-a5bcb76e5c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514888} {:answer/id #uuid "53550687-5bc6-5d71-b56f-d4df929d00c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515053} {:answer/id #uuid "745015e3-e981-5fcb-a0d8-9fc8adee58db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515111} {:answer/id #uuid "de4f9499-4120-5b2b-9957-fb77ce79db15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515338} {:answer/id #uuid "f673d168-be18-50e2-a30f-7e13848f8d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53550687-5bc6-5d71-b56f-d4df929d00c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515053} :answer-field/id #uuid "81cebeb7-8aa0-5776-9a06-8fc04eda14d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6f97d30d-8f92-59ad-aa37-53ebf51b3b4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-143945" :question/problem "In a particular national park, there are three forests. Forest $A$ occupies $40%$ of the park's total area, and $30%$ of the plants it contains are poisonous, while forest $B$ occupies $25%$ of the park's total area, and $20%$ of the plants it has are poisonous. Forest $C$ occupies the remaining area of the park. If the probability that a randomly selected plant from the park is poisonous is $0.24,$ what percentage of plants from forest $C$ are poisonous? Assume that the plants are evenly distributed throughout the park." :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the event that a randomly selected plant is poisonous, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the plant is from forest $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the plant is from forest $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the plant is from forest $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space. \n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected plant is poisonous, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(V) & =P(V\\cap A)+P(V\\cap B)+P(V\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(V|A)P(A)+P(V|B)P(B)+P(V|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.  \n\n- Since forest $A$ occupies $40%$ of the total park land and  forest $B$ occupies $25%$, then forest $C$ occupies the remaining\n\n$$\n100%-(40%+25%)=35%.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.4,\\,P(B)=0.25,\\,P(C)=0.35.\n$$\n\n- Since $30%$ of the plants in forest $A$ and $20%$ of the plants in forest $B$ are poisonous, we have\n\n$$\nP(V|A)=0.3,\\,P(V|B)=0.2\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected plant from the park is poisonous is $0.24,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(V)=0.24.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(V|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(V) & =P(V|A)P(A)+P(V|B)P(B)+P(V|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.24 & =(0.3)(0.4)+(0.2)(0.25)+P(V|C)(0.35) \\\\ 0.24 & =0.12+0.05+0.35P(V|C) \\\\ 0.35P(V|C) & =0.07 \\\\ P(V|C) & =0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $20%$ of plants from forest $C$ are poisonous."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0ebe700-63af-57d4-ae8f-9e09dac5cf76" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional Probability Using the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0af764f3-b723-5507-8469-f93dbd86be3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436145} {:answer/id #uuid "3574dea6-9246-5cae-a2cf-9c3e11c20d81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.78" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436156} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa53c70-acee-5628-a03a-9893e9d3f000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.82" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436175} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd58733-0f63-54ef-9f6c-dd8dcfdc1bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436193} {:answer/id #uuid "cce469ac-4cda-56d9-8c5a-051399a581af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3574dea6-9246-5cae-a2cf-9c3e11c20d81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.78" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436156} :answer-field/id #uuid "6631a042-bc4e-5a30-993a-12ef7f46bef3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14105181-c45b-52bb-8273-83f863c9ff48" :question/math-academy-id "q-143995" :question/problem "A company manufactures NY Yankees caps at three different plants. Plant $A$ produces $36%$ of the caps, of which $25%$ are white, Plant $B$ produces $34%$ of the caps, of which  $40%$ are white, and Plant $C$ produces the remaining $30%$ of all the caps. If the probability that a randomly selected cap from any of the three plants is white is $0.46,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected cap from plant $C$ is white?" :question/worked-solution "Let $W$ be the event that a randomly selected cap is white, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the cap is from plant $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the cap is from plant $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the cap is from plant $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected cap from any of the three plants is white, we can use the law of total probability:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W) & =P(W\\cap A)+P(W\\cap B)+P(W\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|B)P(B)+P(W|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since plant $A$ produces $36%$ of all the caps, plant $B$ produces $34%$ of all the caps, and plant $C$ produces $30%$ of all the caps, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.36,\\,P(B)=0.34,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $25%$ of the caps from plant $A$ and $40%$ of the caps from plant $B$ are white, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|A)=0.25,\\,P(W|B)=0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected cap from any of the three plants is white is $0.46,$ we have \n\n$$\nP(W)=0.46.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(W|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W) & =P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|B)P(B)+P(W|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.46 & =(0.25)(0.36)+(0.4)(0.34)+P(W|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.46 & =0.09+0.136+0.3P(W|C) \\\\ 0.3P(W|C) & =0.234 \\\\ P(W|C) & =0.78\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected cap from plant $C$ is white is $0.78.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0ebe700-63af-57d4-ae8f-9e09dac5cf76" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional Probability Using the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a2bfeb5-10a6-5364-95db-232f11580849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436160} {:answer/id #uuid "534d1982-5794-5ef3-b6c7-550fa347ae5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436165} {:answer/id #uuid "b3684175-dfef-564c-9015-7ac3c04daa84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436191} {:answer/id #uuid "cc71899e-2b08-5805-bb87-08237cd8b8dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436196} {:answer/id #uuid "e29dcf7e-fe90-5acc-b60e-5b9007f23b8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a2bfeb5-10a6-5364-95db-232f11580849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436160} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dda8d7f-6956-55ad-9209-2cf83e277760" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae1e8adc-b949-5f9d-889f-68a0d9d9eba2" :question/math-academy-id "q-144013" :question/problem "Three similar bags, $A,\\,B$, and $C,$ contain balls. Bag $A$ contains $4$ red and $6$ green balls, and bag $B$ contains $6$ red and $4$ green balls. A bag is selected at random, and a ball is randomly selected from the bag. If the probability that the selected ball is green is $\\frac{3}{5},$ what proportion of balls from bag $C$ are green?" :question/worked-solution "Let $G$ be the event of selecting a green ball at random from one of the three bags, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the ball is from bag $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the ball is from $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the ball is from bag $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a green ball, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G\\cap A)+P(G\\cap B)+P(G\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|B)P(B)+P(G|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a bag is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since there are $6$ green balls out of $10$ in bag $A,$ and $4$ green balls out of $10$ in bag $B,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G|A)=\\frac{6}{10},\\,P(G|B)=\\frac{4}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of choosing a green ball is $\\frac{3}{5},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G)=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(G|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|B)P(B)+P(G|C)P(C) \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & =(\\frac{6}{10})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{4}{10})(\\frac{1}{3})+P(G|C)(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{15}+(\\frac{1}{3})P(G|C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(G|C) & =\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{2}{15} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(G|C) & =\\frac{4}{15} \\\\ P(G|C) & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{4}{5}$ of the balls in bag $C$ are green."}]}
